Patient Summary Dashboard — Market Research & Evidence Dossier (India)

Companion evidence base to MRD v3.0 and PRD v1.0 · 24 Oct 2026 · Owner: Product Strategy · Confidential

Evidence grading used throughout: [V-P] verified primary source · [V-S] verified secondary source · [E] reasoned estimate · [H] hypothesis, not yet validated · [Q] open question. This dossier substantiates the claims cited in the one-page MRD and PRD. It deliberately separates what the published record proves (the general clinician information-retrieval burden, the regulatory regime, the interoperability standards) from what remains unproven for this specific product and market (India-specific OPD magnitude, demand, willingness to pay).

1. Problem evidence — is clinician information-retrieval burden real?

The general problem class is among the best-documented findings in health-IT research. What is NOT yet established is the magnitude in Indian private multispecialty OPD specifically, or that a separate summary dashboard is the correct remedy. Both are the subject of the validation sprint.

Finding

Source

Grade

Ambulatory physicians spent 27.0% of the clinic day on direct clinical face time and 49.2% on EHR and desk work; a further 1–2 hours of EHR work after clinic (“pajama time”).

Sinsky C, et al. Allocation of Physician Time in Ambulatory Practice. Ann Intern Med 2016;165(11):753–760.

[V-S]

Primary care physicians spent ~5.9 hours of an 11.4-hour workday on the EHR; clerical and inbox work dominated. Documents the clerical load the summary aims to cut.

Arndt BG, et al. Tethered to the EHR. Ann Fam Med 2017;15(5):419–426.

[V-S]

Physicians spent ~16 minutes per encounter using the EHR; chart review, documentation and ordering were the three largest components. Chart review is the task the dashboard targets.

Overhage JM, McCallie D. Physician Time Spent Using the EHR During Outpatient Encounters. Ann Intern Med 2020;172(3):169–174.

[V-S]

Clinician-reported information retrieval friction during busy Indian OPD sessions; magnitude and frequency in multispecialty private clinics.

No published India-specific OPD time-motion study located. Primary data required.

[H] / [Q]

Interpretation: the literature robustly establishes that clinicians lose substantial time to EHR navigation and that chart review is a large, recurring cost — the exact task a summary view compresses. It does NOT prove the magnitude transfers to Indian private OPD, nor that the bottleneck is screen navigation rather than data fragmentation, data quality, or access restrictions. The refined problem statement (“quick visibility of important patient information during busy periods”) is therefore graded [H], supported by a strong analogous evidence base, pending India-specific time-motion measurement.

